    "finding": "loss_enc = masked row sum / (n p_d): present t2i rows weighted 1/p_d (F60 2.5x, F80 4.4x). The per-peer clip is almost never active (F60 RP frac_clipped 0.0014 at 8000), and AdamW normalises step size per parameter, so the scaling does not make a larger effective lr; encoder grad norms are similar to ROUTE_RANDOM (end window img 0.376 vs 0.437, txt 0.507 vs 0.386). The 3.5x larger parameter distance (0.071 vs 0.021) comes from a consistent gradient direction (collapse toward the layout code), not from the scale."
  },
  "scope_of_impact": "all JOINT units (R39, R39X, R40 encoder arms, R41, R42, R43 joint, R44, R45, R46-B) are evaluated with the same centring; under layout-free centring the R39 F60 ALL J falls 517.9 -> 496.5 (H 505.2, i.e. -8.7 instead of +12.7), R41-C F80 ALL J 457.6 -> 448.1 (H 464.9), R39X F00 ALL J 527.6 -> 520.4 (H 522.1). The JOINT gains reported since R39 are therefore not established; frozen-encoder (head-only) units use the frozen-H cache and are not affected by the student centring (only by the native-evaluation layout effect common to all arms).",
  "caveat": "global / decoupled centring changes the student input statistics. Reference points: the parent (same model, only centring changed) loses 9-11 rSum in its student-cosine readout and 0 in the two-stage readout (its head residual is zero, stage 1 = frozen-H cache); the JOINT units lose 7-21 rSum two-stage (ALL / PA / RR) and 12-88 (ROUTE_PRESENT). How much of the ALL drop is layout leak vs distribution shift is not separated by this audit; a JOINT student trained with layout-free centring (e.g. global or shuffled-batch FIT means) would separate them.",
  "status": "R44 B/C UNDER_AUDIT; no code changed",
